{"id":"storage-volume-health-monitoring","corpus":"k8s","title":"Volume Health Monitoring","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-health-monitoring/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Volume Health Monitoring"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the CSIVolumeHealth feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"CSI volume health monitoring lets a CSI driver report health problems with a volume or with its storage backend directly to Kubernetes. The driver reports through CSI RPCs, and Kubernetes surfaces the reports on three status fields: PersistentVolumeClaim .status.healthStatus, Pod .status.volumeHealth, and CSINode .status.storageHealth. Automation can watch these durable status fields instead of having to reconstruct volume health from ephemeral Events or from vendor-specific dashboards."},{"kind":"p","text":"The CSIVolumeHealth feature gate has existed since Kubernetes v1.21, but the mechanism described on this page is a redesign that replaces the original alpha implementation. See Limitations for what changed."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How it works"},{"kind":"p","text":"The CSI spec defines four RPCs for health reporting:"},{"kind":"li","text":"On the CSI controller plugin: ControllerListVolumeHealth and ControllerGetVolumeHealth, for controller-observed, per-volume health."},{"kind":"li","text":"On the CSI node plugin: NodeGetVolumeHealth, for node-observed, per-volume health, and NodeGetStorageHealth, for the health of the storage backend as seen from that node."},{"kind":"p","text":"A driver only needs to implement the RPCs it wants to support, and advertises support through CSI plugin capabilities. A driver that implements none of these RPCs is never probed, and reporting stays dormant for that driver. A driver that advertises the controller LIST_VOLUME_HEALTH capability must also advertise GET_VOLUME_HEALTH; the csi-external-health-monitor-controller sidecar enforces this requirement."},{"kind":"p","text":"Each health report carries a status drawn from a small, machine-parseable set of values, together with a driver-defined reason and an optional human-readable message:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Volume-level status values (used for PersistentVolumeClaim.status.healthStatus and Pod.status.volumeHealth): Inaccessible, DataLoss, Degraded."},{"kind":"li","text":"Storage-backend status values (used for CSINode.status.storageHealth): StorageUnreachable, StorageDegraded."},{"kind":"p","text":"The node-side and controller-side reports are independent: a volume can be Inaccessible from one node that lost its data path to the backend while the controller plugin still reports the volume as healthy, and vice versa."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Health reported on Pods"},{"kind":"p","text":"For volumes that use a CSI driver supporting the node-side NodeGetVolumeHealth RPC, the kubelet periodically calls that RPC for each CSI volume it has mounted for a Pod, and writes the result to pod.status.volumeHealth, keyed by the volume name from pod.spec.volumes. The probe interval is the kubelet's volumeStatsAggPeriod setting (the --volume-stats-agg-period command line flag)."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet only writes pods/status, a subresource it is already authorized to update for Pods bound to its own node, so no new authorization is required for this field."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Health reported on CSINode"},{"kind":"p","text":"For each CSI driver registered on a node that supports NodeGetStorageHealth, the kubelet periodically calls that RPC and writes the result to csinode.status.storageHealth, keyed by driver name."},{"kind":"p","text":"A StorageHealthCondition entry can optionally scope itself to a specific accessMode or volumeMode, for backends that degrade asymmetrically (for example, a network problem that affects ReadWriteMany access but not ReadWriteOnce)."},{"kind":"p","text":"Writing to csinodes/status is a new capability added by this feature: the Node authorization mode and the NodeRestriction admission plugin only allow a kubelet to patch the CSINode object that matches its own node, and only while the CSIVolumeHealth feature gate is enabled."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Health reported on PersistentVolumeClaims"},{"kind":"p","text":"Controller-observed volume health is written to persistentvolumeclaim.status.healthStatus by the csi-external-health-monitor-controller sidecar that runs alongside a CSI driver's controller plugin. The sidecar calls ControllerListVolumeHealth (or falls back to ControllerGetVolumeHealth per volume) and writes the result:"},{"kind":"p","text":"No node ever writes this field; only the sidecar, running in the control plane, does. This keeps a compromised or misbehaving node from being able to influence what other users of the cluster see on a PVC."},{"kind":"p","text":"Whether this path is available for a given driver depends on that driver, and its deployment of the csi-external-health-monitor-controller sidecar, having adopted the new controller RPCs."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Enabling volume health monitoring"},{"kind":"p","text":"Volume health monitoring is controlled by a single feature gate, CSIVolumeHealth, on both kube-apiserver and the kubelet:"},{"kind":"li","text":"On kube-apiserver, enabling the feature gate allows the new status fields to be written and read; disabling it drops the fields on the next write to the object, while preserving values already stored."}],"lemmas":{"monitoring":["monitoring","monitor"],"monitorings":["monitoring","monitorings"],"monitors":["monitor","monitors"],"monitored":["monitored","monitor"],"features":["feature","features"],"featuring":["feat